# Safety (human-only gates)

Mechanical, never bend, regardless of framing. This is a LIVE production website
(cloudemployee.io). Be meticulous.

## The agent NEVER does these. Jake does them by hand.
- NEVER touch credentials (`.env`, `.env.local`) or set/remove env vars on
  Vercel or in Sanity. Env-var and token changes are an explicit approval gate.
  The `SANITY_MIGRATION_WRITE_TOKEN` carries patch/delete + asset-upload power;
  treat any write-token action as a stop-and-ask.
- NEVER bulk-mutate the live Sanity `production` dataset without sign-off. The
 dataset holds ~388-451 verified docs including the CONTENT-1E embed recovery.
 Stop-and-ask covers: any patch whose GROQ selects more than one document,
 `createOrReplace` on a whole document (it silently drops every field absent
 from the payload), deletes, unsets, and re-running a content migrator. For
 those the agent WRITES the script; Jake reviews and runs it.
- The agent MAY run a NARROW patch: one named document, named fields, values
 sourced from a committed code file, idempotent on re-run. Say plainly which doc
 and which fields before running, and flag that the write overwrites anything
 Seb may have edited in Studio for those fields.
- NEVER fabricate a CE site fact, a Sanity query result, or a content/image
  figure. Specify the probe, wait for Jake's paste (see 20-probes).
- NEVER run the production cutover or activate the 317 redirects without explicit
  approval. LAUNCH is irreversible without a prepared rollback.
- The agent MAY build/deploy code to a Vercel PREVIEW for review. Production
 cutover, domain config, redirect activation, and any env-var change stay Jake's
 by-hand gates.
- The agent MAY run `npx sanity deploy`. A Studio deploy ships SCHEMA CODE to the
 hosted editor; it writes no documents and is reversible by redeploying. Do not
 confuse it with a dataset write just because the roadmap says "deploy Studio +
 seed" in one breath. Deploying is the agent's. Dataset writes follow the narrow
 patch vs bulk/destructive boundary above. Removing or renaming an existing
 field is still a stop-and-ask (it orphans data in live documents).

## Push + commit discipline
- Push ALWAYS waits for Jake's explicit approval. Never push unprompted.
- Commits only at named HALTs. Single-line commit messages, no body, no
  Co-Authored-By trailer. Explicit path staging, never `git add -A` / `git add .`.
- One phase per session. No overclaiming in commit messages; state divergences in
  the DEV-N log, do not hide them.
- Before trusting a build: run typecheck/lint/build manually. There is no CI
  safety net. These rules and Jake's manual gates are the only gate.

## Brief + architecture gates
- Briefs are LOCKED before execution. The execution brain does not change
  architecture mid-build (Two-Brain model, see 00-core). If a build step needs an
  architecture decision not in the brief, STOP and surface it.
- High-stakes briefs (schema, security, foundational, HIGH-complexity) get a
  cross-model audit via the `.audit/` kit before they're finalised. Do not skip
  the audit on a foundational or pre-launch brief to save time.

## Design-restart specific gates
- Do not "rebuild" components that only need a token re-skin. Diff against
  COMPONENTS.md and the new Figma first; tag re-skin / shape-edit / new. A
  rebuild where an edit was correct is wasted spend and lost behaviour.
- Do not transition `migrations.status` off `content_complete` during design or
  template phases.

## When ambiguous
Fail closed. Log, never silent. Saying less and doing less is the safe default.
Probe before claiming. Catch the error before it propagates into permanent docs
or live content.

## Don't-touch list (intentional, documented)
- The CONTENT-1E embed recovery path and its data. Do not re-flatten.
- The Visual Editing draft-mode security order. Do not loosen.
- env.ts client/server split, Finsweet `@2` module patch, over-broad nothing-to-
  tidy scaffold quirks logged as Tech Debt. Defer to SCAFFOLD-AUDIT, don't chase.
- DESIGN-1 Brief B parked Steps 7, 9, 10, 11. Leave parked unless the brief says.
